    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7053A\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the suspen-\nsion of a license to drive a motor vehicle or motorcycle\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nFollowing the enactment of the Driver's License Suspension Reform Act of\n2020, it became apparent that drivers were not able to have their\nlicenses reinstated as was intended under the law. This bill ensures\nthat drivers have the chance to reinstate a license by engaging in an\ninstallment payment plan.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends paragraph (a) of subdivision 3 of section\n226 of the vehicle and traffic law, as amended by section 6 of part J of\nchapter 62 of the laws of 2003, to include entering a payment plan as a\nway to satisfy requirements for a conviction on a failure to appear\nsummons. An acquittal or a dismissal of charges will also satisfy\nrequirements.\n\nSection 2 amends paragraph (a) and the closing paragraph (b) of subdivi-\nsion 3 of section 514 of the vehicle and traffic law, as amended by\nchapter 382 of the laws of 2020 to include entry into an installment\npayment plan.\n\nSection 3 amends paragraph (a) and paragraph (iv) of paragraph (c) of\nsubdivision 4-a of section 510 of the vehicle and traffic law, as\namended by chapter 76 of the laws of 2021 to include entry into an\ninstallment payment plan to satisfy requirements to avoid license\nsuspension.\n\nSection 4 amends paragraph (a) of subdivision 2 of section 1802 of the\nvehicle and traffic law, as amended by chapter 76 of the laws of 2021 to\nallow for payments in installments. Payment plans shall also be offered\nafter conviction.\n\nSection 5 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe intent of the Driver's License Suspension Reform Act was to end the\ndisproportionately harsh impact of driver's license suspensions for\nthose who cannot afford to pay outstanding fines. While failure to\nappear was not included as enacted in the chapter amendment following\nthe bill, it is still the legislature's intent that those with failure\nto appear suspensions may have their license reinstated by engaging with\nan installment payment plan. Drivers should not have to pay in whole to\nreinstate a driver's license. Requiring the entire penalty undermines a\ndriver's ability to earn an income and is thus counterproductive for\nresolving outstanding penalties.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined. Increased engagement in payment plans may increase\nrevenue.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided, however that section 2\nof this act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\nsection 1 of chapter 76 of the laws of 2021 and section 5 of this act\nshall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as section 3\nof chapter 76 of the laws of 2021.\n",
